[2190.952865, "o", "\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m Tests green. Let me confirm the actual `--json` stdout is clean JSON (my one adversarial concern: stdout noise breaking the TUI's parse).\r\n"]
[2193.957625, "o", "\u001b[2m"]
[2193.957923, "o", "│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m \u001b[36mBash\u001b[0m: cd /home/joe/ai/aida-pr-1293 BIN=/home/joe/ai/aida/target/debug/aida echo \"=== done spec (terminal → not-eligible) ===\" …\r\n"]
[2208.722211, "o", "\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m The error cases exit **0** — let me investigate that precisely, since an error path returning success is a contract concern.\r\n"]
[2211.975259, "o", "\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m \u001b[36mBash\u001b[0m: cd /home/joe/ai/aida-pr-1293 BIN=/home/joe/ai/aida/target/debug/aida echo \"=== bad id: separate streams ===\" $BIN zen \"j…\r\n"]
[2279.342627, "o", "\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m That was a test artifact (the `| head` pipe captured `head`'s exit) — the `--json` error path correctly exits **1**. One real (minor) observation surfaced: `probe_drive_gate` reads `out.stderr` for the error text, but the CLI's `--json` error message lands on **stdout**, so the TUI would show a generic \"the gate probe failed\" rather than the specific reason. Impact is low — the probe is only ever called with a real spec id from the focused row, so that error path is essentially unreachable from the drive gesture, and the TUI still safely refuses to launch. Cosmetic, non-blocking.\r\n\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m Let me record verdicts in the worksheet, then execute the headless-orchestrated handshake.\r\n"]
[2290.105507, "o", "\u001b[2m│ [headless:pr-1293]\u001b[0m \u001b[36mBash\u001b[0m: cd /home/joe/ai/aida-pr-1293 cat >> .aida/review-prompt-pr-1293.md <<'EOF'  ---  ## Verdicts (recorded by /aida-review —…\r\n"]
